      "rationale": "The SQL_REGEX table in sqlparse/keywords.py deliberately classifies LIKE, ILIKE and the NOT-prefixed variants with the single rule `(NOT\\s+)?(LIKE|ILIKE|RLIKE)\\b -> tokens.Operator.Comparison`, i.e. the library's documented/intended behavior is that LIKE and ILIKE (with or without NOT) are tokenized identically as Operator.Comparison and are therefore grouped by sqlparse.engine.grouping.group_comparison into a single sqlparse.sql.Comparison node, exactly like any other comparison operator (=, <, etc.). The test reproduces the reported \"like and ilike comparison\" regression by parsing simple `x LIKE y` / `x ILIKE y` / `x NOT LIKE y` / `x NOT ILIKE y` expressions and asserting that (a) the statement groups into exactly one sqlparse.sql.Comparison token and (b) the middle operator token's type is tokens.Operator.Comparison - both of which follow directly from the SQL_REGEX rule shown in the source and from the Comparison class docstring (\"A comparison used for example in WHERE clauses\"). If the regression breaks LIKE/ILIKE classification or grouping, these assertions fail with AssertionError (assert False), matching the report; once the underlying rule/grouping is restored, they pass without touching any other documented behavior.",
